okay today I'm going to talk to you  about series circuits so do this I'm  just going to draw a simple circuit  right here so here is an ammeter here is  one filament lamp here is another  filament lamp and here is another  ammeter  so that's what I'm going to do to begin  with so a series circuit the way I  remember it is s is for series s is for  single because there's just a single  root for the electrons to flow around  there's just a single loop in a parallel  circuit there's more than one way for  the electrons flow around but in a  series circuit just one way for the  electrons to flow around now this means  a huge disadvantage of a series circuit  is if I have two filament lamps and  let's say this one breaks that breaks  the whole circuit that means the  electrons can't flow all the way around  the circuit and as a result that bulb  will also go out as well so that's a big  problem with a series circuit another  issue is the more bulbs we put in the  dimmer each bulb will appear and we'll  see why in just a minute so first of all  let's have a look at these a meters so  an ammeter if you remember measures the  the flow of electrons the currents the  flow of charge around the circuit so  anywhere in a series circuit let's say  if this ammeter here read 10 amps then I  know for a fact that this ammeter here a  2 would also read 10 amps because the  current is the same everywhere in a  series circuit we've just got one way  for the electrons to flow one way for  the charge to flow so as a result it's  going to be the same everywhere where it  gets slightly different is when we start  looking at potential difference so let's  imagine this cell here has a potential  difference of 10 volts if I put in this  voltmeter here first of all if I put a  voltmeter in even though volt meters are  connect  in parallel this circuit is still called  a series circuit because the voltmeter  is just there to read the potential  difference so if I've got two volt  meter's in here one around that filament  lamp lamp one round for that film in  that what is the potential difference  going to be for each so in order to do  this it's kind of a good idea to turn  this on the side  so what we're imagining here is as the  charge goes through our cell the cell is  giving it the energy it's as if it's  lifting it up and given energy all of  that energy needs to be transferred  across these two components and that  energy is going to be shared equally  across all of them so if we've got a 10  volt cell all 10 volts need to be evenly  distributed across V 1 and V 2 of course  the first component and the second  component so if I've got a 10 volt cell  v 1 is going to read 5 volts and V 2 is  also going to read 5 volts this is the  reason why the more bulbs you put in the  dimmer they will be because less energy  will be transferred across each  component so that is how currents and  potential difference works in a series  circuit I'm just going to do a really  simple series circuit just underneath  just to show how resistance works  because resistance is probably the  easiest out of everything and how it  works  so remember resistance is what restricts  the current restricts the flow of charge  around the circuit so if I've got this  resistor here let's say that's 3 ohms  and I've got this resistor here which is  2 ohms in order to find the total  resistance in the circuit all you need  to do is 2 plus 3 which is 5 ohms so to  find the resistance in a series circuit  all you need to do is add the resistance  together to find your total so just to  summarize in a series circuit the  current is the same everywhere the  potential difference is shared across  each component and it would be shared  evenly as long  have the same resistance and finally  resistance is calculated just by adding  together the resistance from the  different resistors and that is the  outline of a series circuit thank you
 

